d0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:underline">attract som=
e Republican support</a><span> in the Senate. And even if Trump refuses to =
sign it, they see a political upside in forcing him to veto broadly popular=
 legislation.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;col=
or:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-style:italic">Can the federal =
government actually overturn results that they don=E2=80=99t like=3F =E2=80=
=94 </span><span>Elena Yee, United States</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-we=
ight:700">Devlin Barrett, who covers the U.S. Justice Department:</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span>No. The Constitution gives the authority to administer elections to=
 the states, not the federal government.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That doesn=E2=80=
=99t necessarily mean the administration won=E2=80=99t try to influence the=
 outcome. In 2020, Trump urged state election officials in Georgia to =E2=
=80=9Cfind=E2=80=9D roughly 11,000 more votes to change the outcome there. =
That effort failed. This year, the administration has filed aggressive laws=
uits seeking to assert greater control over the elections. To date, those s=
uits have been resoundingly unsuccessful; most election experts and lawyers=
 expect the administration=E2=80=99s ongoing legal gambits to continue to f=
ail, even if they reach the Supreme Court.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Constitutio=
n also does not grant the president sweeping emergency powers to affect ele=
ctions. And there are laws on the books designed to ensure access to the po=
lls and to explicitly bar armed federal agents from being present at pollin=
g stations.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"=
