gn of certain victory in 2028.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px =
georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Traditionally, midterm elec=
tions act as referendums on a president=E2=80=99s first two years in office=
=2E These races are no exception. Much of the Democratic strength appears t=
o stem from deep unhappiness with President Trump. Majorities say they disa=
pprove of some of his signature policies, including the Iran war, tariffs a=
nd aggressive immigration enforcement.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>There are some brig=
ht signs for Democrats. Their candidates appear to be winning back Latino v=
oters and young men, who swung to Trump in 2024.</span></p><p style=3D"font=
:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But voter=
s aren=E2=80=99t giving particularly high marks to the Democratic Party. Su=
rveys indicate that there is still dissatisfaction with the party=E2=80=99s=
 national brand. And some of the most successful Democratic candidates are =
working hard to distance themselves from Democratic leaders in Washington. =
So, even if their candidates win, Democrats still have plenty of work to do=
 to persuade Americans to send them back into the White House.</span></p><t=
